WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:14.900
Hello everyone!

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:17.200
How's it going?

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.200
Let me see if I can get
my slide clicker out.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.680
Yeah, this is my first
time in Singapore.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:26.720
Yeah, it's nice.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:31.100
This is already going well.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.200
If I get an applause for that.
That's fantastic.

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:35.900
I haven't really
looked around yet,

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:37.800
like I landed just before the conference,

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.600
so I can't say I've really
experienced it yet,

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.100
but I'm going to stay on for a few days
afterwards, and have a proper explore.

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:45.300
I brought my partner
along with me as well

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:49.480
and I have to say that was
a big mistake, a huge mistake,

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:52.200
because she's been really
looking forward to this trip,

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.000
and especially over the last couple
of weeks she's been saying things like,

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.000
two weeks till we go to Singapore,

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.200
one week till we go to Singapore,

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.200
five days to go, four days to go,

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.550
and I am like, could
you please not do that

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:06.760
because what I'm hearing is five days,

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:08.960
you only have five days
to finish your talk.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.040
Four days, three days,
you're still not ready, are you?

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:16.200
She's like the worst project manager ever just
taunting me on how unprepared I am,

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:18.350
but then – she's brilliant

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:22.800
because she knows how
to put up with me because I stress.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:24.300
I stress pretty easily.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.300
I stress about things that
aren't really problems.

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:30.120
I stress about things that have
really nothing to do with me.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:34.280
Someone posted this picture
on Twitter last week,

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:42.560
He's holding a MacBook between
two fingers above concrete!

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:45.900
I have not slept since I saw this picture.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.000
I stress about code like this, firstly
because there's no semicolons.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.200
Give me a cheer if you use
semicolons in JavaScript.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.360
Yes, good to hear.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:01:59.700
The rest of you are monsters.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:02.700
But that's not the main thing
that stresses me out.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.900
It's because it's adding stuff to the DOM

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:09.100
and then hiding it like presumably
to show it some time later,

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:10.800
like on click or something

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:12.920
and that stresses me out
because it's like,

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.520
can we be sure

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:19.280
that the user's not going to see a flash
of that element before it's hidden

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:22.760
and I've never been able
to re-create this problem,

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:23.760
I've never seen it happen,

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.800
but you never know when it
comes to race conditions.

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:32.360
So, I’d always just swap those lines
around, just so I can get some sleep.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:35.800
But really, there's no race
condition here

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:39.680
because the timing of
running code and rendering

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:44.040
is all tightly defined
and mostly deterministic.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:46.700
And that is thanks to the event loop,

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:49.960
and if I do a half decent job
in the next 30 or so minutes,

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.040
you'll know why things run
in the order they do

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.840
and I don't know,
it might even make sense,

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:57.840
but there's no promises there.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:02.760
So, web pages have a thing that
we tend to call the main thread.

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:05.440
Here's the main thread.

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:09.300
We call it the main thread
because loads of stuff happen here.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:10.500
It's where JavaScript happens.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.100
It's where rendering happens.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.500
It's where the DOM lives.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:18.800
And this means that the bulk of your stuff
on the web has a deterministic order.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:21.600
We don't get multiple bits of code
running at the same time,

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:23.200
like trying to edit the same DOM

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:26.900
and giving you a world of
horrible race conditions.

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:32.000
But it does mean that if something
on the main thread takes a long time

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:34.900
and by a long time I mean
like 200 milliseconds,

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:37.300
that's a long time in terms
of user interaction,

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.000
then it becomes really noticeable

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:41.050
because it's blocked loads
of other things,

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:43.680
like it blocks rendering,
it blocks interaction

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:47.080
and I think it's difficult
for us to think in this way

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:51.960
because as humans we are
extremely multi-threaded,

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:55.400
like I can stand here,
I can wave one hand,

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:57.600
I can stand on one leg,
I can wave the other leg

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:00.550
and all the time I'm speaking,
I'm breathing

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:02.850
and processing audio
and visual information.

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:06.280
As humans we don't really
have a main thread.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:10.560
We don't really have things
that block unrelated things.

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:15.200
I mean we have one
and that is when we sneeze

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:21.080
because as you begin to
sneeze just stuff shuts down.

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:21.880
You know?

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:24.380
The first thing you lose
is the ability to talk,

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:26.780
then you pull a stupid face.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:29.600
If you're driving at this point,
this is where you think

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:34.400
“huh, I hope no one dies,
least of all me.”

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:38.760
And then the human body becomes entirely
single-threaded like you were sneezing.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:40.000
Nothing else.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:42.300
You can't see, hear, think.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:46.700
You can move and make noises but only
in ways the sneeze wants you to,

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:48.700
you have no control over this at all.

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:50.150
And then it's over, right?

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:54.800
You essentially wake up and you
find out if your car is still on the road,

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:57.300
if you have the same number
of limbs you started with,

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:59.300
the same number of passengers
you started with.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:04.560
Needless to say, we don't want to
write code that is like a sneeze.

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:07.200
So, although we have
this main thread thing,

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.200
we tend to spawn
a whole series of threads

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:10.500
like for networking stuff,

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:14.080
encoding and decoding, crypto,
monitoring input devices,

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:17.880
but once these threads have done something
that the page needs to hear about,

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:21.880
they need to sort of come back to the
main thread to give it that information

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:24.700
and it's the event loop
that orchestrates all of this.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:27.320
Take setTimeout, for instance.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:29.320
Is it badly named?
Yes.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:31.120
Are the arguments in the wrong order?

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:32.520
I'd say so.

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:35.400
But, have you thought
about how it actually works?

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:38.800
Well, let's write a web standard for it

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:41.000
because that's what I do these days.

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:42.680
We'll start with...

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:45.760
The setTimeout method, when invoked,
must run the following steps:

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:48.000
Wait ms milliseconds, invoke callback.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:49.000
Done.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:51.960
But this isn't quite working

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:54.640
because spec text like this,

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:57.200
this runs on the same thread
as whatever invokes it,

00:05:57.500 --> 00:05:59.200
and in this case,
it's invoked by JavaScript,

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:00.900
so this is running on the main thread.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:04.480
So, if we say wait
5000 milliseconds,

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.600
we are waiting 5000 milliseconds
on the main thread.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.600
We're blocking lots of other stuff,

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:12.280
so this spec is very sneezy right now,

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:13.280
so we need to change that

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:14.580
and we do this.

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:16.280
We run the steps in parallel

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:19.600
which is magic spec speak
for get off the main thread

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:20.600
or get off this thread

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:23.760
and run this stuff kind of at
the same time as other stuff.

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:26.560
But we create a new problem here

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:30.800
because now we're invoking a callback
from something other than the main thread

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:34.500
and I mean, there's no way
this can really work.

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:37.000
You would end up with lots of
JavaScript running in parallel,

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:38.500
still editing the same DOM

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:40.300
and you'd end up with all
of these race conditions.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:42.300
So, what we do is this,
we queue a task

00:06:42.650 --> 00:06:45.760
and we queue a task to get back on
to the main thread at some point

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:49.080
and now we're calling JavaScript on
the thread where JavaScript lives,

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:50.700
so it all works.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.000
And this is a core part of
how the browser works.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:55.000
If you click the mouse,

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:57.900
like how does that get from the
operating system into your JavaScript?

00:06:57.900 --> 00:06:59.200
It queues a task.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:02.000
When you fetch something how did
you get the response into your JavaScript?

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:03.700
Well, it queues a task.

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:06.000
And you send a message
from a page to a worker,

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.300
once again it queues a task to do that.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:12.100
So, the first part of the event loop
I want to look at are task queues,

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:14.880
and this is the oldest part
of the event loop.

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:17.880
Rather than look at the spec

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:22.600
I thought it might be easier to
try and visualize the event loop.

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:25.900
So, here it is.

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:26.900
This is it.

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:29.720
I hope that clears up
any questions you have.

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:31.480
Actually, I really hope it does

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:34.000
because I based the whole talk
around this one diagram,

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:35.000
so I hope it works.

00:07:35.350 --> 00:07:36.600
But yeah, without anything to do,

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:39.800
the event loop just spins round
and round in a CPU efficient manner.

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:43.920
Now, this visualization is running
at a fraction of a percent of real time,

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:46.500
and it's still kind of too fast
to really see what's going on,

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:48.500
so let's slow things down a bit.

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:52.240
When we queue a task
the event loop takes a detour,

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:55.240
so this here this detour here,
this is where tasks happen.

00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:58.850
So, at some point the browser
says to the event loop,

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:01.000
hey, I've got a job for you to do

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:02.800
and the event loop is like,

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:04.000
excellent, okay,

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:06.600
add it to my to-do list and I'll
get round to it at some point.

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:07.900
No problem.  Done.

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:11.450
What if we do this
like using setTimeout?

00:08:11.450 --> 00:08:16.800
We queue two callbacks that we want
to run after 1 second, 1000 milliseconds.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:20.200
Well, according to the spec we wrote
these two algorithms go parallel,

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:22.760
each waits for a thousand milliseconds

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:25.400
and then they need to come
back on to the main thread,

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:27.000
and they do that by queuing a task.

00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:29.500
So, the browser says to the event loop,

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:32.240
hey, I've got something here that
wants to do main thread work.

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:34.240
In fact, I have two things

00:08:34.540 --> 00:08:38.480
and it adds each one as a separate
to-do item in the task queue.

00:08:38.780 --> 00:08:42.040
The event loop's like,
sure, that's fine, I'll get around to it.

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:44.040
So, it runs the first callback,

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:46.040
it goes around the event loop

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:48.040
and runs the second callback.

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:50.240
And that's tasks.

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:53.920
And it would be pretty
simple if that's all it was,

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:57.640
but it gets more complicated when
we think about the render steps,

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:01.680
and this is what the browser uses to
update what's actually on the screen.

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:04.920
The render steps are another detour

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:06.800
and that involves style calculation.

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:10.000
This is looking at all of
the CSS that's going on

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:12.400
and working out what
applies to each element.

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:15.640
Layout, this is creating a render tree,

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:18.120
figuring out where
everything is on the page

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:20.120
and where it's positioned.

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:24.760
And then creating the actual pixel data,
doing the actual painting.

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:27.100
So, at some point the browser
will say to the event loop,

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:30.000
hey, you know, we need to
update what's on the screen

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:31.750
and the event loop's like no problem,

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:34.280
I'll get around to that next time
I go around the event loop.

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:37.450
Now, I don't know about you,

00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:42.000
but I would consider myself
an expert at coding badly,

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.000
but I can take very
simple bits of JavaScript

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:47.840
and create infinite loops out
of them in places I least expect,

00:09:48.150 --> 00:09:51.500
but let's take a closer look at
what happens when I do that.

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:54.320
Here's a page with a gif and some text

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:57.360
and a big button that runs
an infinite JavaScript loop.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:00.600
So, if I click that button,
everything stops.

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:01.600
The gif has stopped.

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:03.600
I can no longer select text.

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:06.120
The whole tab has kind
of come to a standstill.

00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:07.760
Code for this is simple.

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:10.280
This is just button click while (true).

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:14.280
So, how do we actually visualize this?

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:15.480
Well, the user clicks the button.

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:16.480
So, the browser says,

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:18.280
hey, event loop,
I've got a task for you,

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:20.280
and event loop's like
yep, no problem, I'm on it.

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:23.120
But this task never ends.

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:26.120
It's running JavaScript forever.

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:28.680
A couple of milliseconds
later the browser says,

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:33.000
hey, event loop, like we need to update
that gif that was on the page.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:36.280
So, if you could just render at your
next earliest convenience

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:37.280
that would be fantastic.

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:38.080
The event loop's like,

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:39.640
yeah, okay,

00:10:39.640 --> 00:10:43.720
I'll get around to that right
after I finish this infinite loop

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:45.520
that I'm busy doing right now.

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:48.320
Then the user tries to highlight text

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:50.120
and that involves like hit testing,

00:10:50.120 --> 00:10:52.800
involves looking at the DOM
to see what the text actually is.

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:53.500
So, the browser says,

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:55.960
hey, I've got a couple of more
items for your to-do list there.

00:10:55.960 --> 00:10:57.960
And the event loop's like,
are you having a laugh?

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:01.280
Like do you know how long it takes
to perform an infinite loop?

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:03.980
It's a long time, you know.

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:05.280
There is a clue in the name.

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:09.600
So, that is why a while loop blocks
rendering and other page interaction,

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:11.600
but this is a good thing in practice.

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:13.600
We look again at
the code that I started with.

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:16.200
I used to worry that this would
result in a flash of content,

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:17.500
but it can't, right,

00:11:17.750 --> 00:11:19.500
because this script
runs as part of a task

00:11:19.950 --> 00:11:21.600
and that must run to completion

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:24.240
before the browser can get
back around to the render steps.

00:11:24.900 --> 00:11:28.960
The event loop guarantees your task will
complete before rendering next happens.

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:30.800
It still stresses me out though,

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:33.200
I'll always swap these lines
around when I see it in code.

00:11:34.300 --> 00:11:35.850
So, a while loop blocks rendering,

00:11:35.850 --> 00:11:37.250
but what about this?

00:11:37.750 --> 00:11:39.000
So, this is a loop,

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:40.840
but each time we go round the loop

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:44.040
we're using setTimeout
to queue the next call.

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:45.440
Well, let's find out.

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:47.440
So, very similar testing like before.

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:48.440
I click the button

00:11:49.540 --> 00:11:51.300
and things are still working.

00:11:51.450 --> 00:11:53.500
In fact, it kind of looks
like nothing has changed,

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:55.500
but in the background
here's what's happening.

00:11:56.700 --> 00:11:57.600
We queue a task,

00:11:58.200 --> 00:11:59.600
go around the event loop,

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:00.600
pick up that task

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:02.500
and we queue another
task as a result

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:05.760
and that just keeps happening
and happening until the end of time,

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:10.880
but as we've already seen like only one
task can be processed at a time.

00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:12.000
So, when it's processing a task,

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:15.560
it's having to go all the way around
the event loop to pick up the next task.

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:19.000
So, that means at some
point the browser can say

00:12:19.250 --> 00:12:21.760
huh, we should update
the display for that gif

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:25.200
and it can and it can go around
and update the display,

00:12:25.450 --> 00:12:28.400
and that's why a setTimeout
loop is not render blocking.

00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:32.640
But if you want to run code that has
anything to do with rendering

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:35.700
a task is really
the wrong place to do it

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:39.700
because a task is on the opposite side
of the world to all of the rendering stuff

00:12:39.700 --> 00:12:41.300
as far as the event loop is concerned.

00:12:41.450 --> 00:12:44.480
What we want to do is we want to
run code in the render steps.

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:46.380
I want to run code here.

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:48.380
And the browser lets us do that

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:51.380
and it lets us do that using
requestAnimationFrame.

00:12:51.380 --> 00:12:53.700
Another badly named function, I think,

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:55.700
but it's really good for this purpose.

00:12:57.100 --> 00:12:58.100
RAF callbacks,

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:00.560
they happen as part
of the render steps

00:13:01.460 --> 00:13:02.860
and to show why this is useful,

00:13:02.860 --> 00:13:07.880
I'm going to animate a box,
just a box using this code.

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:10.800
So, I'm going to move
that box forward one pixel

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:14.960
and then use requestAnimationFrame
to create a loop around this

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:17.860
and that's it.

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:18.860
That's all it does.

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:20.860
So, that's requestAnimationFrame,

00:13:20.860 --> 00:13:25.480
but what if we switched
requestAnimationFrame for setTimeout?

00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:29.000
It looks like this.

00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:31.750
Now, this box is moving faster.

00:13:31.750 --> 00:13:33.950
It's moving about 3.5 times faster,

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:36.480
and that means this callback
is being called more often,

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:38.180
and that is not a good thing.

00:13:38.180 --> 00:13:39.680
That's not a good thing at all.

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:44.320
We saw earlier that rendering
can happen in between tasks.

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:48.500
Yes, but just because it can
happen doesn't mean it must.

00:13:48.500 --> 00:13:50.850
In reality we can take a task,
should we render?

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:51.950
No, it can't be bothered yet.

00:13:51.950 --> 00:13:53.950
Go around the event loop,
pick up another task.

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:55.150
Shall we render now?

00:13:55.150 --> 00:13:57.350
No, it doesn't feel
like the right time.

00:13:57.650 --> 00:13:59.350
Many tasks can happen

00:13:59.900 --> 00:14:01.350
and before the browser goes,

00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:04.350
yeah, actually next time
we will update the display

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:07.280
and the browser gets
to decide when to do this

00:14:07.280 --> 00:14:09.280
and it tries to be
as efficient as possible.

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:12.560
The render steps only happen if there's
something actually worth updating.

00:14:12.860 --> 00:14:14.560
If nothing's changed, it won't bother.

00:14:14.900 --> 00:14:18.100
Like if the browser tab
is in the background,

00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:20.100
if it isn't visible, it will
never run the render steps

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:21.300
because there's no point,

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:24.800
but also the majority of screens
update at a set frequency.

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:27.240
In most cases that's 60 times a second.

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:29.440
Some screens go faster,
some screens go slower,

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:31.440
but 60 Hertz is the most common.

00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:34.520
So, if we changed page styles
like a thousand times a second,

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:37.200
it's not going to run the render
steps a thousand times a second,

00:14:37.450 --> 00:14:39.500
it will synchronize
itself with the display

00:14:39.500 --> 00:14:42.480
and only render up to a frequency
the display is capable of.

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:44.100
Usually, 60 times a second.

00:14:44.100 --> 00:14:45.700
Otherwise, it would be a waste of time,

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:49.520
like there's no point rendering
stuff the user will never see,

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:52.320
but that's what
setTimeout is doing here.

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:53.820
It's moving faster

00:14:53.820 --> 00:14:57.600
because it's updating the position of
that box more times than the user can see,

00:14:57.600 --> 00:15:01.160
more times than this display
is capable of showing us.

00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:03.160
Also, so far,

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:07.000
we've been using setTimeout as this
kind of shorthand for "queue a task"

00:15:07.450 --> 00:15:08.100
and it isn't really

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:10.800
because even though we've put
0 milliseconds for the callback

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:14.400
it's more like 4.7 is what the
browser will use as a default.

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:17.000
The spec says the browser
can pick any number to use,

00:15:17.700 --> 00:15:20.480
but in the things I've tested
it seems to be about 4.7.

00:15:21.380 --> 00:15:25.600
There isn't a single method
that just queues a task,

00:15:25.750 --> 00:15:27.700
but we can kind of fake it
using message channels.

00:15:27.700 --> 00:15:29.700
So, I ran a test with that

00:15:31.250 --> 00:15:32.950
and if you're sensitive
to flashing images,

00:15:32.950 --> 00:15:34.240
it might be best to look away now

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:36.240
because that looks like this.

00:15:36.940 --> 00:15:39.460
There're so many tasks happening

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:42.680
that it kind of just looks like the
box is getting a random position.

00:15:42.680 --> 00:15:45.920
We're getting a task every
two-hundredths of a millisecond.

00:15:46.550 --> 00:15:48.720
So, rendering can
happen between tasks,

00:15:49.150 --> 00:15:50.180
but you can have many,

00:15:50.180 --> 00:15:53.960
even tens of thousands of
tasks between renderings.

00:15:55.100 --> 00:15:56.600
Okay, flashing image has gone now.

00:15:57.500 --> 00:16:01.500
Let's imagine each of these is a frame
that is displayed to the user.

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:04.880
So, our rendering steps they happen
at the start of each frame

00:16:04.980 --> 00:16:07.300
and that includes like
style calculation, layout and paint,

00:16:07.380 --> 00:16:09.080
not necessarily all three every time.

00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:11.080
Depends what actually needs updating,

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:12.350
but I like this.

00:16:12.350 --> 00:16:15.700
I like this.
This is very neat and tidy.

00:16:15.700 --> 00:16:17.700
This is a beautiful picture.

00:16:18.200 --> 00:16:21.800
Tasks on the other hand,
they couldn't give a stuff.

00:16:22.100 --> 00:16:24.400
They just kind of appear
anywhere they fancy.

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:28.600
The event loop ensures that
they happen in the right order,

00:16:28.600 --> 00:16:30.100
they happen in the order
they were queued,

00:16:30.100 --> 00:16:32.100
but in terms of
timing within a frame

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:35.080
there is no kind of
ordering here at all.

00:16:36.780 --> 00:16:38.200
And we saw this with our setTimeout.

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:41.560
We were getting four per frame,
three or four per frame

00:16:42.100 --> 00:16:44.240
and that means that
three-quarters of those callbacks

00:16:44.240 --> 00:16:46.800
were wasted effort in terms of rendering.

00:16:46.900 --> 00:16:49.320
Old animation libraries used
to do something like this,

00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:51.820
where they were trying to
use a millisecond value

00:16:51.820 --> 00:16:55.160
that's going to give them
roughly 60 callbacks per second

00:16:56.800 --> 00:16:58.400
and they're assuming
a lot about the screen there.

00:16:58.400 --> 00:16:59.700
They're assuming a screen is 60 Hertz,

00:16:59.700 --> 00:17:00.800
but that was the common case.

00:17:00.800 --> 00:17:04.200
So, it kind of worked,
it eliminated some of the duplicate effort.

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:07.000
Unfortunately, it was a massive hack

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:08.950
because setTimeout was not
designed for animation

00:17:08.950 --> 00:17:09.840
and it really shows

00:17:09.840 --> 00:17:12.440
like due to inaccuracies
you can end up with drift.

00:17:12.540 --> 00:17:15.300
So, what's happened here is we're
doing nothing in one frame,

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:18.000
and then in the next frame we're
doing twice the amount of work,

00:17:18.250 --> 00:17:21.300
and that is a visual jank to user,
it doesn't look great.

00:17:22.150 --> 00:17:23.760
Also, if one of your tasks runs long,

00:17:23.960 --> 00:17:26.760
you can end up moving
the render steps around

00:17:26.760 --> 00:17:28.160
because it's all running
on the same thread

00:17:28.160 --> 00:17:31.160
and you're sort of disturbing
that lovely routine that they have.

00:17:32.300 --> 00:17:35.560
If we use requestAnimationFrame
rather than setTimeout,

00:17:36.050 --> 00:17:37.560
it would look a lot more like this.

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:38.920
All neat and tidy.

00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:40.120
All nice and ordered.

00:17:40.220 --> 00:17:41.760
Everything is within
the timing of the frame,

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:43.760
even this longer task here.

00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:47.700
When I see performance traces
like this, this makes me happy.

00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:50.000
This is showing a good user experience.

00:17:50.150 --> 00:17:51.200
Makes me feel very calm.

00:17:52.420 --> 00:17:54.440
You can't avoid tasks
completely of course

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:56.720
because things like click events

00:17:56.720 --> 00:17:58.080
they're going to be
delivered to you in a task,

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:01.440
and generally you want to respond to
those as soon as possible, fair enough,

00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:03.000
but if you have things like timers

00:18:03.200 --> 00:18:04.800
or you have stuff coming
from the network,

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:07.150
I really recommend using
requestAnimationFrame

00:18:07.150 --> 00:18:08.400
to batch that work together,

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:10.400
especially if you already
have animations running

00:18:10.400 --> 00:18:12.400
because you can save yourself
a lot of duplicate work.

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:17.920
I treat tasks like I treat people
who drink fizzy water,

00:18:18.150 --> 00:18:20.200
like I acknowledge that they exist

00:18:20.550 --> 00:18:22.350
but I keep our interaction
to a minimum

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:25.120
because I do not trust them at all.

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:28.580
I mean I would consider myself
an empathetic person,

00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:30.200
but I have limits,

00:18:30.200 --> 00:18:33.650
like I think soda water
is totally disgusting,

00:18:33.650 --> 00:18:36.050
and I cannot think of a way

00:18:36.050 --> 00:18:39.720
that a human being could drink
fizzy water without gagging

00:18:39.720 --> 00:18:41.740
or being sick or passing out
or something.

00:18:41.740 --> 00:18:44.200
So, when I say someone
who does drink fizzy water,

00:18:44.200 --> 00:18:48.080
I think there must be something
else going on with them.

00:18:48.950 --> 00:18:52.480
I maintain a Twitter list of people
who drink fizzy water.

00:18:55.100 --> 00:18:56.200
It's not a creepy thing.

00:18:56.200 --> 00:18:59.750
I just want to make sure
I know what they're up to

00:18:59.750 --> 00:19:00.750
and what they're doing,

00:19:01.300 --> 00:19:03.800
but when I find the link
between these people

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:07.580
and Brexit and Donald Trump,

00:19:07.580 --> 00:19:09.580
I am blowing the case wide open,

00:19:10.100 --> 00:19:11.580
taking him straight to the FBI

00:19:12.050 --> 00:19:14.250
and it pains me to
tell you this, JSConf,

00:19:14.250 --> 00:19:17.360
but this conspiracy goes
straight to the top.

00:19:18.760 --> 00:19:22.600
I saw him in airport
drinking fizzy water.

00:19:22.750 --> 00:19:24.400
Every now and then someone
will say to me,

00:19:24.400 --> 00:19:26.800
“oh, but Jake, you drink Diet Coke,

00:19:26.800 --> 00:19:29.500
and I think you'll find the main
ingredient is fizzy water,

00:19:29.500 --> 00:19:30.240
ha ha ha.”

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:31.140
No, that's different,

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:36.880
that's completely different like the main
ingredient of air is nitrogen, right?

00:19:37.150 --> 00:19:39.520
But you would still die
if that's all you breathed.

00:19:39.520 --> 00:19:41.000
So, it's more like that.

00:19:41.150 --> 00:19:43.000
You cannot survive on just fizzy water.

00:19:44.350 --> 00:19:45.300
What was I talking about?

00:19:45.300 --> 00:19:47.300
requestAnimationFrame.  Right.

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:49.300
There's one more detail
I want to get to

00:19:49.300 --> 00:19:51.900
and this is something that catches
a lot of developers out,

00:19:51.900 --> 00:19:52.900
it caught me out.

00:19:53.650 --> 00:19:58.800
requestAnimationFrame it comes before
processing CSS and before painting.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:01.700
So, code like this
might seem expensive,

00:20:01.700 --> 00:20:04.400
like we're showing and hiding
a box many many times,

00:20:04.850 --> 00:20:06.000
but this is actually really cheap,

00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:09.880
like JavaScript will always run to
completion before rendering happens.

00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:11.100
So, while you're doing this

00:20:11.100 --> 00:20:12.500
the browser just sits back,

00:20:12.850 --> 00:20:15.280
and it lets you have your
fun changing a value

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:18.520
and it doesn't really think about
it in terms of CSS at all.

00:20:18.900 --> 00:20:21.920
And then at the end when it actually
comes around to the render steps

00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:25.750
it goes, right, what did you
actually change in the end?

00:20:25.750 --> 00:20:27.750
And the only bit that
matters is this final line.

00:20:29.750 --> 00:20:31.200
And this explains a gotcha in CSS

00:20:31.200 --> 00:20:32.600
or at least something
that caught me out.

00:20:32.600 --> 00:20:34.600
I had a thing, right?

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:41.160
that I wanted to animate from
an X position of 1000 to 500.

00:20:42.400 --> 00:20:44.000
And that sounds easy, right?

00:20:44.350 --> 00:20:46.200
So, I had my listener here.

00:20:46.400 --> 00:20:48.200
I set the X position to 1000,

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:49.900
I told it to transition

00:20:50.700 --> 00:20:52.880
and I changed the value to 500,

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:56.600
but that animated from 0 to 500

00:20:56.600 --> 00:21:01.450
and I was like come on, browser,
that isn’t what I asked you to do.

00:21:01.450 --> 00:21:02.240
It's very clear.

00:21:02.240 --> 00:21:04.700
I said 1000 transition to 500.

00:21:04.700 --> 00:21:05.200
What?

00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:11.040
I figured out that maybe I'm giving it
too much information all at once.

00:21:11.700 --> 00:21:14.000
And it's the same
reason we saw before

00:21:14.000 --> 00:21:15.800
like the browser is not
going to think about it

00:21:15.800 --> 00:21:17.800
because I am just to install
one block of JavaScript.

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:20.800
So, it's going to ignore
that first transform value.

00:21:21.550 --> 00:21:22.800
So, I go, okay, fair enough,

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:24.200
what I'll do then is
I'm going to put this, 

00:21:24.200 --> 00:21:26.840
this second bit, I'm going to put
inside a requestAnimationFrame

00:21:27.100 --> 00:21:30.840
and now it still animates from 0 to 500,

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:33.000
and I was like what is going on here?

00:21:33.850 --> 00:21:36.480
Well, I will tell you what's going on
because I finally figured it out.

00:21:36.780 --> 00:21:39.840
The user clicks on the button
and that's a task.

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:40.840
So, we come around to here

00:21:41.440 --> 00:21:44.840
and this is where we set the
initial transform and the transition.

00:21:45.200 --> 00:21:45.840
Fine.

00:21:46.300 --> 00:21:47.840
We queue an animation frame,

00:21:47.840 --> 00:21:48.840
and we go around

00:21:49.150 --> 00:21:52.300
and this is where we set the destination,

00:21:52.300 --> 00:21:54.300
the final transform value.

00:21:55.150 --> 00:21:58.200
But the browser doesn't think
about CSS until this next step over,

00:21:58.200 --> 00:21:59.200
the purple block there.

00:21:59.200 --> 00:22:01.500
This is where it calculates the CSS.

00:22:01.900 --> 00:22:04.800
So, again it totally
misses the first value

00:22:04.800 --> 00:22:08.400
because it hasn't thought about styles
in between those two things being set.

00:22:09.800 --> 00:22:11.000
And that's why to make this work

00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:13.900
you need to use not one
but two requestAnimationFrames

00:22:13.900 --> 00:22:16.880
and now this will animate
from 1000 to 500.

00:22:18.280 --> 00:22:20.280
Incidentally, there is a hacky
alternative to this.

00:22:20.780 --> 00:22:22.500
You can use something
like getComputedStyle

00:22:22.500 --> 00:22:24.100
and just access one of
the properties on it

00:22:24.350 --> 00:22:25.550
and this forces the browser

00:22:25.550 --> 00:22:28.760
to perform style calculation a lot
earlier than it naturally would,

00:22:29.350 --> 00:22:33.100
but it makes the browser take note of all
of the things you set up until that point.

00:22:33.100 --> 00:22:36.550
So, it's like oh, okay,
transform = translateX(1000)

00:22:36.550 --> 00:22:38.600
that's a thing that this element does.

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:40.550
But you need to be
careful with doing this

00:22:40.550 --> 00:22:42.050
because you can end up
forcing the browser

00:22:42.050 --> 00:22:44.850
to do a lot of extra style work
than it really wants to,

00:22:44.850 --> 00:22:46.850
it only really wants to do
that once per frame.

00:22:48.360 --> 00:22:52.240
In reality the best way to deal with
this would be the animation API,

00:22:52.640 --> 00:22:53.750
the web animation API,

00:22:53.750 --> 00:22:56.300
because you can just say I want it
to go from here to this other value

00:22:56.300 --> 00:22:57.300
and it all just works,

00:22:57.600 --> 00:22:59.550
but that's only in Chrome.

00:22:59.550 --> 00:23:02.000
So, it's not really worth
talking about right now.

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:09.700
So, if the position of requestAnimationFrame
within the render steps,

00:23:09.700 --> 00:23:11.000
if that was a surprise to you,

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:12.600
if that was something
you didn't know already,

00:23:13.400 --> 00:23:14.600
it's probably not your fault.

00:23:14.600 --> 00:23:17.250
You might have been misled
by particular implementations

00:23:17.250 --> 00:23:19.300
because Edge and Safari,
they get this very wrong,

00:23:19.300 --> 00:23:21.000
they put RAF around about here,

00:23:21.350 --> 00:23:23.520
most notably they put it after paint.

00:23:24.350 --> 00:23:25.320
And that's kind of annoying

00:23:25.320 --> 00:23:28.560
because it means like if the user clicks
somewhere or something happens,

00:23:28.760 --> 00:23:32.000
and you want to batch that work you've
been using requestAnimationFrame,

00:23:32.500 --> 00:23:36.100
Edge and Safari they will render
before they get to your callback.

00:23:36.100 --> 00:23:37.800
So, the user is going to see something

00:23:38.800 --> 00:23:41.160
and that means that you're not going
to see the actual changes you make

00:23:41.300 --> 00:23:43.160
until the next frame along

00:23:43.160 --> 00:23:46.680
and that's adding quite a significant
delay to things appearing on the screen

00:23:47.150 --> 00:23:49.700
and it also makes it really difficult
to batch work together.

00:23:50.500 --> 00:23:51.700
I hope this is something they fix soon.

00:23:51.700 --> 00:23:53.700
There's been activity
on the bugs recently,

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:55.700
but the web standards
say it should be here,

00:23:55.700 --> 00:23:58.640
and that's where it is in
Firefox and in Chrome.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:01.840
Okay, that's enough about
requestAnimationFrame.

00:24:02.240 --> 00:24:03.880
I want to take a look at microtasks.

00:24:04.480 --> 00:24:07.150
This is probably the least understood
part of the event loop I'd say.

00:24:08.150 --> 00:24:11.600
I strongly associate
microtasks with promises,

00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:13.900
but this is not where they started.

00:24:14.350 --> 00:24:15.440
Back in the 1990s,

00:24:15.440 --> 00:24:18.320
browsers wanted to give developers
a way to monitor DOM changes

00:24:18.620 --> 00:24:21.440
and the W3C went, okay,
we'll sort that out for you,

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:23.540
and they gave us mutation events.

00:24:23.540 --> 00:24:24.720
So, this is where I could say,

00:24:24.720 --> 00:24:28.360
okay, I want to know when a node
is inserted into the body element.

00:24:28.460 --> 00:24:29.960
And fine, excellent,

00:24:29.960 --> 00:24:31.960
and you get a series
of other events as well.

00:24:33.450 --> 00:24:35.150
But, in practice this
was pretty problematic.

00:24:35.150 --> 00:24:36.550
We take this bit of code here,

00:24:36.550 --> 00:24:40.760
what I'm doing is I'm adding a
hundred spans into the body element.

00:24:41.660 --> 00:24:45.280
How many events would you expect
to receive as a result of this?

00:24:45.800 --> 00:24:46.580
One event?

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:48.780
One event for the whole operation?

00:24:49.300 --> 00:24:50.000
Nope.

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:52.000
100 events?

00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:53.300
One for each span.

00:24:53.300 --> 00:24:54.000
Yes.

00:24:54.500 --> 00:24:57.360
But also, another hundred
for this line here

00:24:57.360 --> 00:25:00.000
when the content is going
into the actual span,

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:01.700
a text node is going into the span

00:25:01.850 --> 00:25:03.200
and because these events bubble,

00:25:03.200 --> 00:25:06.400
this simple piece of code is going
to land you with 200 events.

00:25:07.100 --> 00:25:10.000
And because of this like relatively
simple DOM modifications

00:25:10.300 --> 00:25:12.400
ended up triggering
thousands of events

00:25:12.400 --> 00:25:15.200
and if you were doing like a tiny
bit of work in these listeners

00:25:15.200 --> 00:25:17.200
that quickly became a big bit of work,

00:25:17.200 --> 00:25:18.700
and it was a performance disaster.

00:25:19.500 --> 00:25:22.700
What we really wanted was a way to
sort of hear about a batch of this work.

00:25:22.700 --> 00:25:24.400
It's similar to what we
saw with styles before.

00:25:24.400 --> 00:25:26.400
We want the browser
to kind of sit back,

00:25:26.400 --> 00:25:28.000
let us do some stuff

00:25:28.100 --> 00:25:31.450
and then at a convenient point, say,
some stuff changed.

00:25:31.500 --> 00:25:35.360
Here is a kind of an event or something
to represent all of those changes.

00:25:36.650 --> 00:25:38.880
We want to hear about
it once not 200 times

00:25:38.880 --> 00:25:40.380
and the answer became mutation observers,

00:25:40.380 --> 00:25:43.080
and they created a new
queue called microtasks.

00:25:43.780 --> 00:25:46.240
A lot of documentation
I read about microtasks

00:25:46.540 --> 00:25:50.600
suggests that it happens like,
I don't know, every turn of the event loop

00:25:50.600 --> 00:25:53.640
or it happens after a task
or something like that

00:25:53.940 --> 00:25:55.640
and it is kind of true.

00:25:56.100 --> 00:25:58.640
There is a single place on the event
loop where microtasks happen,

00:25:58.640 --> 00:26:00.840
but that is not where you'll
generally encounter it.

00:26:01.840 --> 00:26:06.080
They also happen whenever
JavaScript finishes executing.

00:26:06.400 --> 00:26:10.080
That means that the JavaScript stack
has gone from having stuff in it

00:26:10.480 --> 00:26:12.080
to having no stuff in it

00:26:12.900 --> 00:26:14.480
and that's where we run microtasks.

00:26:14.580 --> 00:26:17.800
So, you can end up with microtasks
happening halfway through a task,

00:26:17.800 --> 00:26:22.320
you can have microtasks in the render
steps as part of requestAnimationFrames,

00:26:22.320 --> 00:26:24.320
kind of anywhere,
anywhere JavaScript can run.

00:26:25.820 --> 00:26:27.860
So, that means this JavaScript
will run to completion

00:26:28.150 --> 00:26:29.860
adding a hundred spans
and their contents.

00:26:30.200 --> 00:26:31.600
JavaScript finishes executing

00:26:31.600 --> 00:26:33.600
and we get our mutation
observer callback.

00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:36.400
Promises made use of them as well.

00:26:36.400 --> 00:26:41.000
So, here we queue a microtask
and then log(‘Yo!’),

00:26:41.500 --> 00:26:43.000
JavaScript has finished executing.

00:26:43.850 --> 00:26:46.200
So, we go for the microtasks,
and we log(‘Hey!’).

00:26:47.000 --> 00:26:49.280
And that means when the
Promise callback is executing

00:26:49.380 --> 00:26:53.560
you were guaranteed that no other
JavaScript is midway through at the time,

00:26:53.760 --> 00:26:55.800
the Promise callback is right
at the bottom of the stack,

00:26:55.800 --> 00:26:57.800
and that's why promises use microtasks.

00:26:59.100 --> 00:27:01.400
But what happens if we create
a loop using microtasks?

00:27:01.400 --> 00:27:03.400
A bit like we did with setTimeout before.

00:27:04.600 --> 00:27:05.400
Same demo again.

00:27:06.800 --> 00:27:07.600
Click the button

00:27:08.430 --> 00:27:09.700
and it blocks rendering,

00:27:09.700 --> 00:27:12.240
it blocks the tab in the same
way a plain while loop did,

00:27:12.340 --> 00:27:14.240
very different from setTimeout before.

00:27:15.740 --> 00:27:18.480
So, Promise callbacks are async,

00:27:18.980 --> 00:27:19.580
fine,

00:27:19.580 --> 00:27:21.000
but what does async actually mean?

00:27:21.200 --> 00:27:25.800
I mean all it means is that they happen
after synchronously executing code,

00:27:25.800 --> 00:27:29.240
so that's why we get (‘Yo!’)
before (‘Hey!’).

00:27:29.800 --> 00:27:32.700
But just being async doesn't
mean it must yield to rendering,

00:27:32.700 --> 00:27:35.700
doesn't mean it must yield to any
particular part of the event loop.

00:27:37.100 --> 00:27:39.120
We've looked at three
different queues so far.

00:27:39.120 --> 00:27:40.520
We’ve looked at task queues,

00:27:40.720 --> 00:27:42.050
animation callback queues

00:27:42.050 --> 00:27:44.560
which is where requestAnimationFrame
callbacks happen

00:27:44.560 --> 00:27:46.000
and now we're looking at microtasks,

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:48.720
and just to make your
lives a little bit easier

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:52.000
they all are processed
very subtly differently.

00:27:52.100 --> 00:27:53.600
Like we've seen with task queues,

00:27:53.800 --> 00:27:56.000
we take one item
and we take one item only

00:27:56.000 --> 00:27:58.840
and if another item is queued
it just goes to the end of the queue.

00:27:58.840 --> 00:27:59.400
Fine.

00:28:00.400 --> 00:28:04.040
Animation callbacks they
happen until completion,

00:28:04.840 --> 00:28:08.760
except ones that were queued while
we were processing animation callbacks.

00:28:08.900 --> 00:28:10.760
They are deferred to the next frame.

00:28:11.300 --> 00:28:14.280
Microtasks on the other hand they
are processed to completion

00:28:14.380 --> 00:28:17.040
including any additionally queued items.

00:28:17.240 --> 00:28:20.440
So, if you were adding items to the queue
as quickly as you're processing them

00:28:20.440 --> 00:28:22.440
you are processing microtasks forever.

00:28:23.150 --> 00:28:27.000
The event loop cannot continue until
that queue has completely emptied

00:28:27.200 --> 00:28:29.000
and that is why it blocks rendering.

00:28:29.800 --> 00:28:31.700
I get really excited about this stuff.

00:28:31.700 --> 00:28:34.640
I hope other people are
excited about this as I am.

00:28:35.640 --> 00:28:36.640
Thank you.

00:28:36.640 --> 00:28:38.640
One person.  Excellent.

00:28:39.440 --> 00:28:42.760
You know what, I used to have
a real job like many of you did.

00:28:43.850 --> 00:28:47.760
This stuff like it's sort of speaking
the standards work, the creating slides.

00:28:47.760 --> 00:28:49.060
This used to be my hobby

00:28:49.760 --> 00:28:51.680
but then my hobby became my job,

00:28:52.200 --> 00:28:53.780
and now I have no hobbies.

00:28:54.480 --> 00:28:56.440
I'm a boring person now,

00:28:56.440 --> 00:29:00.200
and I didn't actually notice when
this happened, like genuinely.

00:29:00.200 --> 00:29:02.840
The first time I noticed is
when I went for an eye test

00:29:03.540 --> 00:29:06.720
and the optician just
making small talk asked,

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:09.520
“and what are your hobbies?”

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:15.360
I am like, “oh shit, when did this happen? 
I don't have any.” 

00:29:16.000 --> 00:29:17.060
Like I said, I stress,

00:29:17.060 --> 00:29:20.280
so I got a bit like oh, I can't
say nothing, who says nothing?

00:29:20.800 --> 00:29:22.800
So, it's just totally true.

00:29:22.800 --> 00:29:26.320
I panicked and I said
“I play the piano.” 

00:29:26.620 --> 00:29:28.320
I don't play the piano.

00:29:29.720 --> 00:29:32.480
And then I stressed even more because
I thought like she was going to say,

00:29:32.700 --> 00:29:34.000
“Oh well.  That's great. 

00:29:34.300 --> 00:29:36.680
We don't need to use
the letters chart then,

00:29:36.680 --> 00:29:38.180
here's some sheet music.

00:29:38.880 --> 00:29:41.680
Can you read me the first five bars?
What chord is this?”

00:29:42.420 --> 00:29:43.480
Thankfully she didn't.

00:29:43.480 --> 00:29:46.200
But yeah, my optician
now thinks I'm a pianist.

00:29:46.200 --> 00:29:47.200
That’s great.

00:29:47.450 --> 00:29:51.000
I don't go back to the opticians anymore.
I'm terrified of like more piano chat.

00:29:51.650 --> 00:29:53.300
I don't know how I survive
in the real world.

00:29:55.350 --> 00:29:56.550
I think you're ready for this next one.

00:29:57.650 --> 00:29:59.760
Occasionally I run
little JavaScript quizzes.

00:30:00.300 --> 00:30:01.260
Maybe that's my hobby.

00:30:01.500 --> 00:30:02.500
Nope.  That's work too.

00:30:02.500 --> 00:30:03.200
Never mind.

00:30:03.200 --> 00:30:06.200
Yes, I run little JavaScript quizzes,
and this is my favorite question.

00:30:06.250 --> 00:30:09.240
So, I've got a button
and on click I resolved a Promise

00:30:09.240 --> 00:30:11.240
and then log something.

00:30:12.600 --> 00:30:16.960
But I have two event listeners on
the same element doing the same thing.

00:30:18.200 --> 00:30:21.440
So, if the user clicks a button,
what happens?

00:30:21.440 --> 00:30:23.440
In which order are things logged?

00:30:26.340 --> 00:30:29.100
Well, our first listener executes.
Great.

00:30:29.100 --> 00:30:30.500
So, that's on the JavaScript stack.

00:30:30.900 --> 00:30:32.000
Queues a microtask

00:30:32.180 --> 00:30:34.500
and then we get to the next
line where we log Listener 1

00:30:34.500 --> 00:30:35.700
and that's the first answer.

00:30:37.150 --> 00:30:39.840
Most folks agree on that bit,
but what next?

00:30:39.940 --> 00:30:41.840
And I ran a Twitter poll
on this last week.

00:30:41.840 --> 00:30:43.840
I've been speaking to a few of you,
a few of you saw it.

00:30:43.840 --> 00:30:47.760
Most people would say the next
thing logged is Listener 2, that's 63%,

00:30:47.960 --> 00:30:51.120
5% of people think NaN
is just logs and then Infinity,

00:30:51.650 --> 00:30:52.700
that is not the answer,

00:30:52.700 --> 00:30:54.700
but fair enough to that 5% of people,

00:30:54.700 --> 00:30:56.700
but yes, we've got 63%

00:30:57.100 --> 00:30:59.400
and Listener 2 is the wrong answer.

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:01.700
This is a real gotcha with script,

00:31:02.150 --> 00:31:06.000
but if you thought it was Listener 2
then you're in very good company.

00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:07.000
So, don't worry about it.

00:31:08.550 --> 00:31:09.840
Our Listener has finished.

00:31:10.400 --> 00:31:13.880
So, we've gone from having something
on the JavaScript stack to nothing.

00:31:14.680 --> 00:31:16.760
So, it is Microtask time.

00:31:16.860 --> 00:31:18.200
We are going to run that promise

00:31:18.200 --> 00:31:20.200
and we are going to log Microtask 1

00:31:20.200 --> 00:31:21.000
and there we go.

00:31:21.950 --> 00:31:23.440
And then it's time for
the second listener

00:31:23.440 --> 00:31:24.640
and that works the same.

00:31:24.640 --> 00:31:26.400
So, the order is Listener 1 Microtask 1,

00:31:26.400 --> 00:31:27.700
Listener 2 Microtask 2.

00:31:29.300 --> 00:31:30.900
But that's if the user clicks the button.

00:31:30.900 --> 00:31:33.560
What if the button is
clicked using JavaScript?

00:31:34.560 --> 00:31:36.600
Oh, yes, it's different.

00:31:37.900 --> 00:31:40.680
For starters, our script is on the stack.

00:31:40.780 --> 00:31:45.200
We call click and that synchronously
dispatches the events.

00:31:45.200 --> 00:31:47.200
So, we start with Listener 1.  Great. 

00:31:47.650 --> 00:31:49.960
We queue a microtask and we log Listener 1

00:31:50.100 --> 00:31:51.300
and now it's microtask time.

00:31:51.300 --> 00:31:53.100
No.  No, it is not microtask time.

00:31:53.100 --> 00:31:54.400
We can't run microtasks.

00:31:54.400 --> 00:31:55.400
This is where it's different

00:31:55.600 --> 00:31:57.600
because our JavaScript
stack is not empty,

00:31:57.750 --> 00:31:59.900
button.click has not returned yet.

00:32:00.750 --> 00:32:02.500
So, we move on to Listener 2.

00:32:02.500 --> 00:32:04.000
We queue another microtask

00:32:04.150 --> 00:32:05.600
and now we log Listener 2

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:07.600
and this is where it diverges.

00:32:08.800 --> 00:32:09.800
And now, our listener is done.

00:32:09.800 --> 00:32:11.340
In fact, all our listeners are done.

00:32:11.440 --> 00:32:12.450
button.click returns,

00:32:12.450 --> 00:32:13.900
our JavaScript stack empties

00:32:13.900 --> 00:32:15.800
and now we can process
those microtasks

00:32:15.800 --> 00:32:16.700
and they happen in order

00:32:17.850 --> 00:32:19.600
and this has real-world implications.

00:32:19.600 --> 00:32:23.400
So, beware if you're using
promises in this way,

00:32:23.400 --> 00:32:25.880
if you're using automated tests as well

00:32:25.880 --> 00:32:29.580
because automated tests if they're
clicking things on the page,

00:32:29.580 --> 00:32:31.580
they're likely to be using
JavaScript to do it

00:32:31.580 --> 00:32:33.780
and that can change
the behavior of your code.

00:32:34.580 --> 00:32:35.820
This also came up when
we were looking at

00:32:35.820 --> 00:32:37.820
how to add observables to the DOM

00:32:37.820 --> 00:32:39.620
and how they'd integrate
with promises.

00:32:39.720 --> 00:32:40.620
We hit this question,

00:32:41.300 --> 00:32:46.080
If we have a promise that represents
the next click of a particular link,

00:32:46.280 --> 00:32:47.580
it's just this little bit of code here,

00:32:48.200 --> 00:32:53.080
can someone use that promise
and still call event.preventDefault?

00:32:54.180 --> 00:32:55.280
Promises are async.

00:32:56.100 --> 00:32:58.440
So, have we missed our chance
to prevent the default here?

00:32:58.440 --> 00:33:00.640
Turns out no, it's fine.
It's totally fine.  This works.

00:33:00.640 --> 00:33:04.880
This just works unless again
the user clicks the link

00:33:04.880 --> 00:33:07.720
or some code clicks
the link using JavaScript.

00:33:07.720 --> 00:33:10.440
And this is the final puzzle of the talk.
I'm overrunning a little bit.

00:33:10.640 --> 00:33:13.000
To figure this out we actually
need to take a look at the spec.

00:33:13.420 --> 00:33:17.120
So, this is a very rough description of
how the spec for clicking a link works,

00:33:17.450 --> 00:33:19.320
but we start by creating an event object 

00:33:20.000 --> 00:33:21.320
and then for every listener we have

00:33:21.620 --> 00:33:24.200
we invoke that listener
with the event object

00:33:24.700 --> 00:33:25.900
and then we take a look at,

00:33:25.900 --> 00:33:29.640
has that eventObject's
canceled flag been set.

00:33:29.800 --> 00:33:31.640
If it's been set then we're not
going to follow the hyperlink,

00:33:31.640 --> 00:33:34.240
but if it's unset,
we will follow the hyperlink.

00:33:34.740 --> 00:33:36.240
When you call event.preventDefault

00:33:36.240 --> 00:33:38.880
it sets this canceled flag
on the event object.

00:33:39.880 --> 00:33:41.280
So, if the user clicks
a link that's fine,

00:33:41.280 --> 00:33:44.100
our microtasks happen here
after each callback

00:33:44.100 --> 00:33:46.100
because that's where
the JavaScript stack empties,

00:33:46.100 --> 00:33:47.900
but when we call
click with JavaScript,

00:33:47.900 --> 00:33:52.160
it's just going to call out to
Process a link click algorithm,

00:33:52.700 --> 00:33:55.000
and it only returns once
that algorithm is complete.

00:33:55.000 --> 00:33:59.160
So, the JavaScript stack never
empties during this algorithm.

00:33:59.160 --> 00:34:01.160
So, our micro tasks can't happen.

00:34:02.000 --> 00:34:04.440
So, it hits this step 3
where it looks at the eventObject

00:34:05.150 --> 00:34:08.920
and even if you've got loads of promises
trying to call like preventDefault

00:34:09.820 --> 00:34:10.920
it's too late.

00:34:11.220 --> 00:34:12.520
It's going to follow the hyperlink

00:34:12.520 --> 00:34:15.000
and then sometime later those
promise callbacks happen,

00:34:15.300 --> 00:34:16.500
but you've missed the boat.

00:34:16.500 --> 00:34:19.000
You've missed the point where you
can actually cancel that event.

00:34:20.500 --> 00:34:23.480
So, remember that microtasks
they behave quite differently

00:34:23.480 --> 00:34:25.080
depending on the JavaScript stack.

00:34:26.680 --> 00:34:27.380
Okay.

00:34:27.580 --> 00:34:29.450
So, that 34 minutes,

00:34:29.450 --> 00:34:30.680
that was a massive brain dump

00:34:30.680 --> 00:34:32.400
of everything I know
about the event loop

00:34:32.400 --> 00:34:34.400
and the various steps
and various queues.

00:34:34.600 --> 00:34:36.000
I have found that
knowing this stuff

00:34:36.450 --> 00:34:38.000
like it prevents that case

00:34:38.000 --> 00:34:40.680
where you kind of got a bit of code
that's not doing what you want.

00:34:40.680 --> 00:34:42.000
So, you just wrap
it in a setTimeout

00:34:42.000 --> 00:34:43.600
and now it kind of works sometimes.

00:34:43.900 --> 00:34:45.700
Knowing the stuff kind
of helps avoid that.

00:34:45.700 --> 00:34:47.000
It helps me avoid jank

00:34:47.100 --> 00:34:50.240
by getting stuff running on the correct
part of the event loop as well,

00:34:50.540 --> 00:34:53.520
and I hope I’ve managed to explain this
stuff in a way that is helpful to you too.

00:34:53.720 --> 00:34:55.320
Like I said I'm a stressful person.

00:34:55.320 --> 00:34:58.480
So, as a result of this I'm now
going to go and collapse in a pile,

00:34:58.480 --> 00:34:59.680
but you've been a great audience.

00:34:59.880 --> 00:35:00.680
Thank you very much.

00:35:00.680 --> 00:35:01.180
Cheers!

